    id: "020f3a24da1ac1245ddf",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Who is the 'hero' Indian pilot who was stabbed on Israel-bound flight?",
    summary: "Capt Smit Machchhar was attacked by another pilot onboard the Flydubai flight bound for Tel Aviv.",
    preview: "The pilot who was stabbed on an Israel-bound flight on Wednesday has been identified as Indian national Capt Smit Machchhar. Machchhar was attacked by another pilot onboard a Flydubai flight bound for Tel Aviv, forcing the plane to divert to Saudi Arabia. The captain, who has been hailed as a \"true hero\" by Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, was taken to a hospital in Tabuk, a city in north-western Saudi Arabia.",
    body: "The pilot who was stabbed on an Israel-bound flight on Wednesday has been identified as Indian national Capt Smit Machchhar.\n\nMachchhar was attacked by another pilot onboard a Flydubai flight bound for Tel Aviv, forcing the plane to divert to Saudi Arabia.\n\nThe captain, who has been hailed as a \"true hero\" by Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, was taken to a hospital in Tabuk, a city in north-western Saudi Arabia.\n\nDescribing the events, Machchhar said he was on the floor and still being attacked when he realised the plane carrying more than 170 passengers was in a dive and he had to do something.\n\n\"I was lying on the floor injured but I told myself to go for one last push to open the door from inside,\" he told Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi during a video call on Friday.\n\nYears of experience\n\nMachchhar grew up in the Indian city of Mumbai, where his parents still live. In 2008, he joined Mainland Air, an aviation college in New Zealand's Dunedin, before joining Indian airline SpiceJet as a commercial pilot three years later.\n\nHe moved to Dubai and joined Flydubai in 2022 as an experienced pilot.\n\nPhilip Kean, the CEO of Mainland Air, told the BBC he remembered Machchhar as a man who would remain calm in a crisis.\n\n\"We trained him for forced landing - not this kind of situation, which is very, very rare,\" Kean said. \"It was a long time back but he came across as a good and honest worker.\"\n\nMachchhar has more than 9,750 hours of flying experience, including more than 6,400 hours as pilot-in-command, according to his LinkedIn profile.\n\nHis former employer SpiceJet issued a statement calling Machchhar a \"hero\" who had saved \"countless lives\".\n\nAn old friend who worked with him at SpiceJet, Capt Dharav Gosalia, described him as outgoing and \"very jovial\".\n\nMachchhar - whose nickname was \"Smit Mac\" - was a \"disciplined flier\" who had undergone years of training, he said, including from former Indian Air Force pilots who had moved into commercial aviation.\n\nLast year, Machchhar spoke about how he started flying and his experiences as a pilot on a podcast called Bona-Fide Banter.\n\nAviation, he said, was \"never a childhood dream\". When he was around 19, he joined a management course, planning to work for his family's business. But then he came across an aviation seminar and \"the bug bit [him]\", he added.\n\nMachchhar also spoke about how, when he wore his pilot's uniform, he was \"no-nonsense\" and thought \"about work 100%\". But unlike his younger days, he added, he now placed a greater value on having a healthy work-life balance and spending time with family.\n\n\"Give personal life equal importance - it will help you fly longer,\" he advised.\n\nHe also detailed challenges he had faced in the air, including when a passenger went into early labour and the crew worked together to ensure the baby was born safely.\n\n\"In aviation terms, I was lucky to grow up with people who helped me be the pilot that I am today,\" he said, crediting senior pilots with teaching him how to face stressful situations.\n\nAt the time of the conversation, Machchhar said that no plane he'd flown had needed to make an emergency landing. He had, however, faced medical emergencies and minor aircraft issues that had caused delays.\n\nHe said delays were \"not my problem. As long as you're walking out of the aircraft [safely], that's what I want.\"\n\nIndian social media has been awash with praise for Machchhar since his identity emerged, with commenters calling him a hero. Snippets from the podcast have also gone viral.\n\nStabbed mid-flight\n\nFlydubai's flight FZ1073 took off from Dubai International Airport in the United Arab Emirates at 03:05 GMT (07:05 local time) en route to Ben Gurion Airport in Israel.\n\nThe flight was carrying at least 150 people, with Netanyahu saying \"almost all\" passengers were Israeli.\n\nThe Israeli prime minister later wrote on X that Machchhar had \"saved the lives of 174 people\" and prevented \"a catastrophic mid-air disaster\".\n\nData from flight tracking website Flightradar24 shows the plane made a rapid descent over Saudi Arabia around two and a half hours after take-off, dropping more than 17,000ft (5,000m) in less than two minutes at around 05:21.\n\nMinutes later, the plane signalled an emergency code - squawk 7700 - before switching it to a squawk 7500 code, which indicates a hijacking.\n\nAt 05:40, the plane - which had briefly entered Jordanian airspace - looped back into Saudi Arabia, where it later started its manual descent for landing.\n\nLess than an hour later, the plane returned to squawk 7700.\n\nInitial local media reports cited Israeli officials as saying a fight had broken out between two pilots.\n\nBut speaking on Wednesday after the incident, Netanyahu confirmed later reports that one pilot had stabbed the other before \"apparently trying to crash\" the plane.\n\nHe said an Israeli passenger told him \"the plane went into a spin and began to descend\" before the passenger and a crew member broke into the cockpit and overpowered the \"pilot who had carried out the stabbing\".\n\nThe plane was diverted to Tabuk Airport in north-western Saudi Arabia and touched down at 06:58, according to location data.\n\nThe stabbed pilot, who has since been airlifted to Abu Dhabi, was later identified as Machchhar. The Indian embassy in the United Arab Emirates said he was \"in good health and recovering well\".\n\nIn his call with Prime Minister Narendra Modi, Machchhar said he had been flying planes for 17 years and \"can tell what is happening to the plane even with my eyes closed\".\n\nSpeaking about his recovery, the pilot said he is \"looking at it as a journey\" - saying that it \"will take time\" but he is \"confident that the recovery will be there and I will become fit and healthy again\".\n\nModi and Trump praise Machchhar\n\nIndian Prime Minister Narendra Modi said Machchhar \"showed immense courage and an unwavering resolve to protect the lives of others\" despite being seriously injured.\n\n\"His valour helped save hundreds of lives and avert a great tragedy,\" he wrote in a post on X, adding that he hoped for the pilot's speedy recovery.\n\nAnd during their call, Modi told Machchhar that \"his courage, patience and quick decision-making saved the lives of so many people\" and the whole country was proud of him.\n\nIndian Foreign Minister S Jaishankar praised the pilot's \"extraordinary courage and remarkable presence of mind, even in the face of grave danger\".\n\nAmbassador Deepak Mittal said he was \"proud of Capt Smit for his bravery\" and \"very reassured\" to meet him.\n\nUS President Donald Trump told reporters at the White House that \"the pilot was in very bad shape\" but had the sense to open the cockpit door during the attack.\n\n\"Amazing, in a way he was a hero, to open the door. He was able to fall back and get the door open.\"\n\nAdditional\n\n- Published1 day ago",
